WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:07.800 --> 00:00:09.881
Lindsay Levin: We’ve just
heard from Maersk,

00:00:09.881 --> 00:00:11.883
just one of the players
that you deal with.

00:00:11.925 --> 00:00:13.552
Why is a center like yours needed?

00:00:13.552 --> 00:00:16.930
Bo Cerup-Simonsen: Because we're looking
at a massive systemic change.

00:00:16.930 --> 00:00:19.599
It’s huge, and it needs to happen fast.

00:00:19.599 --> 00:00:21.852
No country, no company,

00:00:21.893 --> 00:00:25.981
no matter how large and powerful,
can do this alone.

00:00:25.981 --> 00:00:30.151
So collaboration is going to need
to be a part of the solution.

00:00:30.193 --> 00:00:32.487
And our center is all about that.

00:00:32.487 --> 00:00:35.740
It is all about collaborating
across the ecosystem,

00:00:35.782 --> 00:00:37.909
across the value chain

00:00:37.909 --> 00:00:40.704
in order to accelerate the transition.

00:00:42.372 --> 00:00:46.626
What Maersk is doing
is absolutely exemplary,

00:00:46.626 --> 00:00:50.213
but few companies have the resources

00:00:50.255 --> 00:00:53.633
and have the courage
to do what Maersk is doing.

00:00:54.551 --> 00:00:56.887
So you can say it's moving,

00:00:56.887 --> 00:01:01.558
it's scaling too slowly, when you look
at the industry-wide perspective.

00:01:01.558 --> 00:01:05.604
So what we really need to do
is to turn those first-mover activities,

00:01:05.645 --> 00:01:07.647
those leading activities,

00:01:07.689 --> 00:01:10.150
into industry-wide practices.

00:01:10.191 --> 00:01:12.360
And so this is what
we're doing in our center,

00:01:12.360 --> 00:01:13.904
this is what we're aiming to do.

00:01:13.945 --> 00:01:16.823
And when the center was established,
about three years ago,

00:01:16.823 --> 00:01:20.368
with a large philanthropic donation
from the A.P. Moller Foundation,

00:01:20.410 --> 00:01:25.040
the basic idea was that we need to create
a completely new way

00:01:25.081 --> 00:01:27.542
of collaborating across the ecosystem.

00:01:28.168 --> 00:01:33.465
The basic idea was that we need
to establish an independent center

00:01:33.506 --> 00:01:35.967
that would have no vested interest.

00:01:36.509 --> 00:01:42.223
We need to collaborate with governments
and with companies across the globe,

00:01:42.223 --> 00:01:44.100
across the ecosystem,

00:01:44.142 --> 00:01:47.479
and we wanted to establish
a team co-located

00:01:47.479 --> 00:01:51.524
so that economists and engineers and so on
would sit side by side

00:01:51.566 --> 00:01:54.486
and imagine the future
and develop the solutions.

00:01:54.527 --> 00:01:55.946
So this was the idea.

00:01:55.987 --> 00:02:01.076
And of course there were a lot of concerns
about compliance and antitrust

00:02:01.117 --> 00:02:05.163
and IP and confidentiality
and practicalities.

00:02:05.163 --> 00:02:06.873
But we have found a model now,

00:02:06.915 --> 00:02:08.959
we have overcome those challenges.

00:02:08.959 --> 00:02:13.546
And now, today, we have more
than 100 organizations across the globe

00:02:13.588 --> 00:02:14.965
joining the center.

00:02:14.965 --> 00:02:18.301
Many are sending people to
our team in Copenhagen.

00:02:18.468 --> 00:02:20.929
Competitors are sitting side by side

00:02:20.929 --> 00:02:23.056
to develop the solutions of the future.

00:02:23.098 --> 00:02:24.808
LL: So that's fabulous.

00:02:24.808 --> 00:02:28.812
Give us a sense of where
and why we're still lagging.

00:02:28.812 --> 00:02:31.064
BCS: Well, if you look three
or five years back,

00:02:31.106 --> 00:02:33.441
I think we were lagging
on the technology side,

00:02:33.483 --> 00:02:35.944
we were lagging on the commercial side,

00:02:35.986 --> 00:02:38.405
we were lagging on the regulatory side.

00:02:38.446 --> 00:02:41.199
But over just the past few years,

00:02:41.199 --> 00:02:43.493
and you just saw
an amazing example of that,

00:02:43.493 --> 00:02:47.247
we've seen tremendous development
in the space of technology.

00:02:47.247 --> 00:02:50.500
So now, just over the next
one to two years,

00:02:50.542 --> 00:02:53.420
we will see container ships,
like what you just saw,

00:02:53.461 --> 00:02:55.171
we'll see tanker vessels,

00:02:55.213 --> 00:02:59.467
big bulk carriers,
cruise ships, car carriers

00:02:59.467 --> 00:03:03.847
being built and getting in the water,
running on new types of fuels.

00:03:03.888 --> 00:03:08.476
For example, methanol, methane,
ammonia a little later.

00:03:08.518 --> 00:03:10.520
So this is now all maturing,

00:03:10.520 --> 00:03:13.857
and the technical readiness
level is really high.

00:03:13.898 --> 00:03:17.402
So the big focus now
is on the commercial readiness,

00:03:17.402 --> 00:03:21.031
on the upscaling opportunity
and the barriers for that.

00:03:21.031 --> 00:03:24.034
And there, we're looking
at two categories of drivers

00:03:24.075 --> 00:03:27.328
and also two areas
that we have to focus on.

00:03:27.370 --> 00:03:32.792
And one is a purely market-driven one
where a customer demands a green product,

00:03:32.834 --> 00:03:35.211
a car, for example, an electric vehicle,

00:03:35.253 --> 00:03:37.088
and that has to be produced.

00:03:37.130 --> 00:03:40.717
And that supply chain needs
to work in a green manner.

00:03:40.759 --> 00:03:44.471
So that is driving now
a part of this transition.

00:03:44.471 --> 00:03:45.680
As we just heard,

00:03:45.680 --> 00:03:49.976
it's just that on the industry-wide scale,
it's not moving fast enough.

00:03:50.602 --> 00:03:52.395
But then we have the regulatory side,

00:03:52.395 --> 00:03:54.814
and here we've just had
a major breakthrough

00:03:54.856 --> 00:03:57.442
in the International
Maritime Organization.

00:03:57.484 --> 00:04:02.906
So just about a week ago,
all 175 countries came together.

00:04:02.906 --> 00:04:04.074
They agreed.

00:04:04.115 --> 00:04:05.950
We had Asia with China,

00:04:05.992 --> 00:04:08.870
we had Europe, we had US, we had Africa.

00:04:08.912 --> 00:04:12.373
Everybody agreed that we need
to update our strategy.

00:04:12.373 --> 00:04:14.709
We need global regulation for shipping,

00:04:14.751 --> 00:04:17.921
we will go to zero by around 2050,

00:04:17.962 --> 00:04:20.673
we will use sound
technical measures to go there,

00:04:20.673 --> 00:04:23.885
and we will use economic measures
like a carbon pricing.

00:04:23.885 --> 00:04:27.806
So that is now a global framework
that has been agreed across the globe.

00:04:27.806 --> 00:04:31.101
So it's a fabulous fundament
for moving forward from here.

00:04:31.142 --> 00:04:34.896
LL: Yeah, so congratulations
to all of those involved in that.

00:04:34.896 --> 00:04:36.064
(Applause)

00:04:36.648 --> 00:04:39.526
What's still difficult
and what are the opportunities?

00:04:40.026 --> 00:04:42.028
BCS: So it's still difficult going forward

00:04:42.070 --> 00:04:45.365
because we're looking at a massive
change on a systemic level

00:04:45.365 --> 00:04:48.076
and a massive change on individual level.

00:04:48.118 --> 00:04:50.870
So a shipowner is looking
into an uncertain future.

00:04:50.912 --> 00:04:54.666
It's much easier to quantify
the additional cost

00:04:54.666 --> 00:04:55.875
and the additional risk

00:04:55.917 --> 00:04:59.546
than it is to quantify
the additional opportunities

00:04:59.587 --> 00:05:02.173
or the risk of not doing anything.

00:05:02.215 --> 00:05:05.176
So that means a lot of companies

00:05:05.218 --> 00:05:08.596
and a lot of countries
are stuck with this uncertainty

00:05:08.638 --> 00:05:10.807
and are hesitant to move forward.

00:05:10.849 --> 00:05:12.267
A ship owner will be hesitant

00:05:12.308 --> 00:05:15.520
because she doesn't know
which fuel type to bet on

00:05:15.562 --> 00:05:17.355
or if the customers will pay.

00:05:17.397 --> 00:05:20.108
A country, for example,
a developing country, is hesitant

00:05:20.150 --> 00:05:24.195
because it's concerned about increased
cost of imported goods

00:05:24.237 --> 00:05:28.283
or how export competitiveness
will influence workplaces and so on.

00:05:28.324 --> 00:05:30.326
So this is a lot of uncertainty in there.

00:05:30.326 --> 00:05:35.165
And this is where we work as a center
to address all those uncertainties

00:05:35.165 --> 00:05:37.292
and those risks and remove those

00:05:37.333 --> 00:05:40.211
so that decision makers can move forward.

00:05:40.753 --> 00:05:44.215
LL: So finally, give us an example
of a specific thing

00:05:44.215 --> 00:05:47.719
that the center is doing to help affect
that kind of change.

00:05:48.094 --> 00:05:50.138
BCS: So one of the great things,

00:05:50.138 --> 00:05:54.058
or one of the things that we've had
a lot of interest in recently,

00:05:54.100 --> 00:05:56.895
is to work with green corridors.

00:05:56.895 --> 00:06:02.775
A green corridor is the realization
of that new business system.

00:06:02.817 --> 00:06:06.196
So we work, for example,
a lot with the Chilean ministries

00:06:06.196 --> 00:06:09.157
and we've worked a lot
with stakeholders in Chile

00:06:09.199 --> 00:06:13.077
to understand how Chile
can produce renewable energy,

00:06:13.119 --> 00:06:15.163
how they can turn that into fuels,

00:06:15.205 --> 00:06:17.582
how they can retrofit their ports,

00:06:17.624 --> 00:06:21.836
how ship-owning companies
can transport using green fuels,

00:06:21.878 --> 00:06:23.213
and how, for example,

00:06:23.254 --> 00:06:27.842
the Chilean mining industry
can transport copper

00:06:27.884 --> 00:06:30.011
with zero carbon transportation

00:06:30.011 --> 00:06:33.973
so that they can export green copper
that can be used in, for example,

00:06:33.973 --> 00:06:35.934
electric vehicles in the future,

00:06:35.975 --> 00:06:40.396
and thereby support the production
of these green supply chains.

00:06:40.396 --> 00:06:44.317
So green corridor is really getting
all those stakeholders together

00:06:44.359 --> 00:06:46.110
across the entire chain.

00:06:46.110 --> 00:06:49.113
It is reimagining,
you can say, the future,

00:06:49.155 --> 00:06:51.449
putting that new business system in place,

00:06:51.491 --> 00:06:54.577
defining how that new business,
what it looks like.

00:06:54.619 --> 00:06:56.704
And we are right now,
for example in Chile,

00:06:56.704 --> 00:06:59.290
brought that all the way forward
to feasibility study.

00:06:59.332 --> 00:07:01.084
So it's very concrete now.

00:07:01.084 --> 00:07:04.963
And then we take that learning
and we turn it into a general blueprint

00:07:04.963 --> 00:07:08.216
and we share that with the world
so that every region,

00:07:08.216 --> 00:07:12.512
everybody can go in and use this cookbook
for how to develop a green corridor.

00:07:13.096 --> 00:07:14.973
And I think this is our way of working.

00:07:15.014 --> 00:07:18.643
We try to identify the decision makers
that can really make this move.

00:07:18.685 --> 00:07:23.356
And then we work specifically
with those leaders, both on company level,

00:07:23.398 --> 00:07:26.859
so this is typically at strategy,
leadership level,

00:07:26.901 --> 00:07:30.154
or in country government level,

00:07:30.196 --> 00:07:34.867
to understand what do they need
in order to move forward.

00:07:34.867 --> 00:07:36.703
So in addition to the green corridors,

00:07:36.744 --> 00:07:39.789
we've developed a number
of guidelines and services,

00:07:39.789 --> 00:07:42.458
how a ship owner
should invest in new ships,

00:07:42.500 --> 00:07:46.921
how a country should support
the development of future regulation, etc.

00:07:46.963 --> 00:07:49.340
So this is our way of working.

00:07:49.799 --> 00:07:53.052
LL: Fabulous, Bo, thank you very much
for the work that you're doing.

00:07:53.094 --> 00:07:56.514
I know that there's going to be
a lot of people here in other industries

00:07:56.514 --> 00:07:58.308
that want to learn from your example.

00:07:58.308 --> 00:07:59.684
Thank you for being with us.

00:07:59.684 --> 00:08:00.977
BCS: Thank you very much.

00:08:01.019 --> 00:08:02.603
(Applause)

